{
 "lead": "A point operation changes a pixel using only its own value. The same curve s = T(r) is applied to every pixel, independent of neighbours. It exists because many images are too dark, too bright, or have a range too wide for a display. The negative, log and power-law curves are the basic shapes; contrast stretching is a piecewise-linear extension. Phone camera exposure sliders, medical X-ray display and Fourier-spectrum viewers all use these maps.",
 "prereq": "You need the 8-bit image model from Unit 1: integer pixel values 0 to 255, L = 256 levels, and normalised values r/255 in 0 to 1.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Point operation and the lookup table",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "An image is an array of pixels. In an 8-bit grey image each pixel is an integer r from 0 to 255, where L = 256 is the number of levels. A point operation, also called an intensity transformation, computes the output pixel s from r alone: s = T(r). The function T is fixed for the whole image. It does not look at neighbours, so a 12-megapixel photo needs the same curve at every point. Identity is T(r) = r."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A lookup table (LUT) makes this fast. Compute T(0) to T(255) once, store 256 bytes, then for each pixel read table[r] and write s. For RGB, apply the same table to R, G and B separately unless you are changing only intensity in HSI."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "s = T(r)",
     "where": "s is the output intensity 0–255; r is the input intensity 0–255; T is the fixed mapping curve."
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "Build a 256-entry table: table[r] = T(r) for r = 0, 1, 2, ..., 255.",
      "Read the next pixel value r.",
      "Set the output pixel to table[r].",
      "Repeat for every pixel and every colour channel if needed."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Negative and log transforms",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The negative is the simplest curve: s = L − 1 − r. For 8-bit data, s = 255 − r. It reverses brightness: black becomes white. It is used to display a faint mammogram or a dark film scan because the eye reads dark detail on a bright background better. The shape is a straight line with slope −1."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The log transform is s = c log(1 + r). Here log is natural log (ln), c is a scale constant. For 8-bit values choose c = (L − 1)/ln(L) = 255/ln(256) ≈ 45.99. Then r = 0 gives s = 0 and r = 255 gives s = 255 exactly. The curve rises fast at low r and flattens near the top, so it lifts dark pixels and compresses a wide range. Fourier spectra are the classic user: one bright DC value can be millions of times larger than the weak high-frequency terms, and the log view makes all frequencies visible."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The inverse-log (exponential) curve does the opposite: it expands bright values and leaves dark values low. A normalised form is s = (e^(a r) − 1)/(e^a − 1), with a > 0 controlling steepness. It is used when highlights, not shadows, carry the detail. A log curve with too large a scale constant c can exceed 255 and clip; choose c so T(255) = 255."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Power law and contrast stretching",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Power-law, or gamma, transformation is s = c r^γ, with r normalised to 0 ≤ r ≤ 1. For 8-bit integers use s = 255 c (r/255)^γ. γ (gamma) is a positive number; c is a scale factor, usually 1 when the input is already normalised. For γ > 1 the curve lies below the identity: bright values are pulled down and the bright range is expanded, so a washed-out image gains contrast in the highlights. For γ < 1 the curve lies above the identity: dark values are raised, shadows are lifted, and the dark range is expanded, so a dark image brightens. γ = 1 is identity."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Contrast stretching is a piecewise-linear curve. Pick two control points (r1, s1) and (r2, s2), with r1 < r2 and s1 < s2. Map r ≤ r1 to s1, r ≥ r2 to s2, and map the middle by a straight line. Values below r1 and above r2 are clipped to the display range. An image whose histogram sits in 40 to 180 can be stretched to 0 to 255 this way. Choosing the points from a histogram is the link to <a href=\"#histogram-processing-equalization-and-specification\">histogram processing</a>."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "s = s1 + (s2 − s1)(r − r1)/(r2 − r1)",
     "where": "for r1 < r < r2; below r1 use s1, above r2 use s2."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "figure": {
  "after_section": 1,
  "caption": "On an 8-bit image the negative maps 64 to 191, the log curve s = 45.98 ln(1 + r) maps it to 192 and γ = 0.4 maps it to 147. Both curves lift the dark pixels and keep 255 at 255.",
