 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "An 8-bit image has pixel values 0, 64, 128, 192 and 255. Apply (a) the negative, (b) the log transform s = 45.98 ln(1 + r), (c) the power law with γ = 0.4 and c = 1. Give the outputs rounded to integers.",
   "steps": [
    "Use L = 256, so the 8-bit maximum is L − 1 = 255.",
    "Negative: s = 255 − r. For r = 0, 64, 128, 192, 255 the outputs are 255, 191, 127, 63, 0.",
    "Log: s = 45.98 ln(1 + r). r = 0 gives 45.98 ln 1 = 0. r = 64 gives 45.98 ln 65 = 191.94 ≈ 192. r = 128 gives 45.98 ln 129 = 223.45 ≈ 223. r = 192 gives 45.98 ln 193 = 241.98 ≈ 242. r = 255 gives 45.98 ln 256 = 254.96 ≈ 255.",
    "Gamma 0.4: s = 255(r/255)^0.4. r = 0 gives 0. r = 64 gives 255(64/255)^0.4 = 146.68 ≈ 147. r = 128 gives 255(128/255)^0.4 = 193.55 ≈ 194. r = 192 gives 255(192/255)^0.4 = 227.64 ≈ 228. r = 255 gives 255.",
    "The log curve pushes the low values 64 and 128 far up while the top stays near 255. That is dynamic-range compression. Check that the log curve maps r = 255 exactly to 255 because c was chosen for that."
   ],
   "answer": "Negative outputs: 255, 191, 127, 63, 0. Log outputs: 0, 192, 223, 242, 255. Gamma 0.4 outputs: 0, 147, 194, 228, 255.",
   "check": "import math\nrs = [0, 64, 128, 192, 255]\nneg = [255 - r for r in rs]\nlog = [round(45.98 * math.log(1 + r)) for r in rs]\ngam = [round(255 * (r / 255) ** 0.4) for r in rs]\nfor v in neg + log + gam:\n    print(v)",
   "values": [
    255,
    191,
    127,
    63,
    0,
    0,
    192,
    223,
    242,
    255,
    0,
    147,
    194,
    228,
    255
   ],
   "output": ""
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Gamma on raw values: computing 255·(r)^γ with r = 64 gives an enormous number (about 1346 for γ = 0.4), not the intended 147. Use r/255 inside the power and multiply by 255 after.",
  "Missing c in s = c log(1 + r): with c = 1 an 8-bit output is at most 5.5 and appears black. For 8-bit use c = 255/ln 256 ≈ 45.99.",
  "Top endpoint: for increasing compression curves such as log and power-law, choose c so T(255) = 255; the negative deliberately maps 255 to 0. Check the endpoint for the specific curve before writing the LUT.",
  "Negative on colour: in HSI invert intensity I only; in RGB invert each channel separately. Mixing them gives wrong hue.",
  "Piecewise contrast stretch: keep control points ordered r1 < r2 and s1 < s2; reversed points make a falling curve and invert contrast."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Define a point operation in image enhancement.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "It computes each output pixel s from the same pixel's input r by a fixed map s = T(r), without looking at neighbours."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why is the log transform used on a Fourier spectrum?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "A spectrum has a huge dynamic range: the DC term can be millions of times larger than weak high-frequency terms. The log curve compresses this range so the weak terms become visible on a display."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "For an 8-bit image, apply the negative to r = 37.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "s = 255 − 37 = 218."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "For an 8-bit image with r = 100, γ = 0.5 and c = 1, compute the power-law output.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "s = 255(100/255)^0.5 = 255 × 0.62622 = 159.7, so s ≈ 160."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "An image histogram occupies only 0 to 70. Which gamma value brightens it and expands its range? Explain with one number.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "Use γ < 1, for example γ = 0.4. The top value maps to about 255(70/255)^0.4 = 152, so the occupied range grows from 0–70 to 0–152 and dark detail is lifted."
  },
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "State one reason to precompute a 256-entry lookup table for a point operation.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "It replaces a log, power or other calculation at every pixel with one array read, so the whole image takes one pass."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "The log maps 64→192 and 128→223. Why does the extra input from 64 to 128 add only 31 output levels, while 0→64 adds 192?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "The slope flattens as input grows. From 0 to 64 the output rises by 192; from 64 to 128 it rises by only 223 − 192 = 31. The value 128 still ends at 223 (95 above the identity), but the marginal gain is smaller."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. Display gamma, sRGB and camera tone curves are power laws with measured exponents. HDR imaging uses log and tone-mapping curves to fit a huge range onto a screen. Medical viewing software uses window/level, a piecewise-linear cousin of contrast stretching. Search for “gamma correction”, “tone mapping” and “histogram equalization” to go further."
